            "theoryHtml": "\n        <h4>The Historical Standoff at Panipat (April 1526 AD)</h4>\n        <p>In November 1525 AD, Babur marched from Kabul with an elite expeditionary force of 12,000 veteran soldiers. He crossed the Indus, annexed Punjab, disarmed the treacherous Daulat Khan Lodi at Lahore, and marched through Ambala toward Delhi. Hearing of the invasion, Sultan <strong>Ibrahim Lodi</strong> advanced from Agra with a colossal army of <strong>1,00,000 soldiers and 1,000 armored war elephants</strong>. The two armies faced each other on the historic, dusty plains of <strong>Panipat</strong> (Haryana) in mid-April 1526 AD.</p>\n\n        <h4>Babur's Tactical Defensive Masterpiece: The Rumi Formation</h4>\n        <p>Recognizing that his 12,000 men were vastly outnumbered by Ibrahim's 1,00,000-strong host, Babur spent eight days constructing a masterly defensive-offensive battle layout borrowed from the <strong>Ottomans (the Rumi formation)</strong>:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Natural Flank Anchors:</strong> Babur anchored his extreme right wing on the fortified walls and buildings of the town of Panipat. On his left flank, he dug a deep trench strewn with felled thorny acacia trees to prevent Afghan cavalry outflanking.</li>\n            <li><strong>The Araba (Carts) Barricade:</strong> In the central front line, Babur tied <strong>700 baggage carts (Araba)</strong> together using twisted rawhide bullock ropes. Between every two carts, five or six protective wooden mantlets (<em>Turah</em> / movable wooden shields) were erected.</li>\n            <li><strong>Gaps for Counter-Attack:</strong> At intervals of every 60 to 70 yards, Babur left open passageways wide enough for 100 to 150 cavalrymen to charge through simultaneously when given the signal.</li>\n            <li><strong>The Gunpowder Arm:</strong> Stationed behind the wooden mantlets were revolutionary gunpowder units:\n                <ul>\n                    <li><strong>Ustad Ali Quli:</strong> Commanded the heavy bronze field cannons (<em>Top / Zarb-zan</em>) placed in the center.</li>\n                    <li><strong>Mustafa Rumi:</strong> Commanded the matchlock musketeers (<em>Tufangchis</em>) deployed on the right-center.</li>\n                </ul>\n            </li>\n            <li><strong>The Tulghuma Wings:</strong> On the extreme left and right wings, Babur stationed elite mobile divisions of mounted archers for the <strong>Tulghuma</strong> (flanking encirclement).</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>The Battle: April 21, 1526 AD</h4>\n        <p>At dawn on Friday, <strong>April 21, 1526 AD</strong>, Ibrahim Lodi launched a headlong frontal assault with his entire massed host:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li>As the Afghan masses approached Babur's lines, they were surprised and checked by the unexpected barricade of chained carts and trenches. The forward ranks stopped abruptly, while the rear divisions continued pushing forward, compressing 1,00,000 men into a chaotic, immobile human jam.</li>\n            <li><strong>The Cannonade:</strong> Ustad Ali Quli's heavy cannons and Mustafa Rumi's muskets opened fire with deafening thunder and thick smoke. The war elephants, having never experienced gunpowder explosions, panicked in terror, threw off their riders, and bolted backward into their own tightly packed infantry lines, trampling thousands underfoot.</li>\n            <li><strong>The Tulghuma Encirclement:</strong> On Babur's command, his flanking cavalry wheeled around the Afghan flanks and rear, raining deadly armor-piercing composite-bow arrows into the trapped Afghan mass from all four directions.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>The Destruction of the Lodi Army and Death of Ibrahim</h4>\n        <p>Surrounded on all sides and unable to maneuver or deploy their numbers, the Afghan soldiers were methodically slaughtered. By midday (barely six hours after fighting commenced), the Sultanate army had ceased to exist:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Between <strong>15,000 and 20,000 Indian soldiers lay dead</strong> on the field.</li>\n            <li>Sultan Ibrahim Lodi refused to flee, fighting heroically with a devoted band of 5,000 warriors until he fell surrounded by heaps of slain comrades.</li>\n            <li>Babur immediately dispatched Prince <strong>Humayun</strong> to occupy Agra, while marching in person to seize Delhi. On Friday, April 27, 1526, the Khutba was read in the Jami Masjid of Delhi in the name of <em>Zahiruddin Muhammad Babur Padshah Ghazi</em>, inaugurating the Mughal Empire.</li>\n        </ul>\n        ",
