            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Humayun’s Clashes with Sher Khan: Battles of Chausa & Kannauj</h3>\n            <p>While Humayun spent months in Bengal celebrating at Gaur (which he renamed <em>Jannatabad</em>), Sher Khan consolidated his hold over Bihar and Jaunpur, severing Humayun’s communication and supply lines with Agra and Delhi.</p>\n            \n            <h4>1. The Battle of Chausa (June 26, 1539 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Strategic Location:</strong> Fought near Buxar on the banks of the Karmanasa River.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Conflict:</strong> After months of tactical standoff and insincere negotiations, Sher Khan launched a pre-dawn surprise assault on the encamped Mughal troops.</li>\n                <li><strong>Humayun’s Escape:</strong> The Mughal army broke into panic. Humayun plunged into the swollen Ganga on horseback and was rescued from drowning by a humble water-carrier named <strong>Nizam</strong> (using an inflated goatskin or <em>mashak</em>). In gratitude, Humayun later granted Nizam the royal prerogative of sitting on the imperial throne in Agra for half a day, during which Nizam issued leather coins.</li>\n                <li><strong>Assumption of Sovereign Title:</strong> Following this victory, Sher Khan formally crowned himself sovereign, adopting the royal title <strong>Farid-ud-Din Sher Shah</strong>, striking coins and ordering the <em>Khutba</em> read in his name.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Battle of Kannauj / Bilgram (May 17, 1540 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Final Confrontation:</strong> Humayun mobilized an army of over 40,000 soldiers, joined reluctantly by his brothers Askari and Hindal. However, internal factionalism and heavy torrential rains waterlogged the Mughal heavy artillery park.</li>\n                <li><strong>Decisive Afghan Victory:</strong> Sher Shah's mobile Afghan cavalry outmaneuvered the immobile Mughal lines without Humayun even managing to fire his heavy cannons.</li>\n                <li><strong>Mughal Flight:</strong> The rout was absolute. Humayun fled westward across the Indus River into the deserts of Sindh and Rajasthan, marking the complete temporary eclipse of the Mughal Empire and the inception of the <strong>Second Afghan Empire</strong> (Sur Dynasty).</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
