            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Humayun’s 15-Year Exile, Safavid Alliance, Restoration & Death</h3>\n            <p>Between 1540 and 1555, Humayun endured a dramatic wandering exile that took him through Sindh, Rajasthan, and eventually to the Safavid court of Persia.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Wanderings in Sindh and Birth of Akbar (1540–1543 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Marriage to Hamida Banu Begum:</strong> While wandering in Sindh in 1541, Humayun married the 14-year-old Hamida Banu Begum, daughter of Sheikh Ali Akbar Jami (the spiritual preceptor of Prince Hindal).</li>\n                <li><strong>Birth of Akbar at Umerkot:</strong> On October 15, 1542, Hamida Banu gave birth to Jalaluddin Muhammad Akbar at the fortress of Umerkot (in modern Sindh, Pakistan), under the hospitable asylum of the Hindu ruler <strong>Rana Prasad</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Safavid Asylum and Diplomatic Alliance (1544 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Court of Shah Tahmasp I:</strong> Finding no loyalty among his brothers in Kabul, Humayun traveled to Herat and Isfahan to seek military assistance from the Safavid ruler <strong>Shah Tahmasp I</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Safavid Treaty Terms:</strong> Shah Tahmasp agreed to equip Humayun with 14,000 elite Persian Qizilbash cavalry under the condition that Humayun outwardly embrace Shi'a court etiquette and surrender the strategic border fortress of <strong>Kandahar</strong> to the Persian crown upon capture.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Reconquest of Kabul and Northern India (1545–1555 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Reclaiming Kabul and Kandahar:</strong> With Persian support, Humayun captured Kandahar and Kabul (1545), eventually subduing his rebellious brothers Kamran (who was blinded and sent on Hajj to Mecca) and Askari.</li>\n                <li><strong>Collapse of Sur Dynasty:</strong> Following the death of Sher Shah's capable son Islam Shah Suri in 1554, the Sur Empire fragmented into internal civil war among rival claimants (Adil Shah, Ibrahim Suri, and Sikandar Suri).</li>\n                <li><strong>Battles of Machhiwara & Sirhind (1555 AD):</strong> Aided by his brilliant general <strong>Bairam Khan</strong>, Humayun invaded Punjab, won the Battle of Machhiwara (May 1555) and routed Sikandar Shah Suri at the decisive <strong>Battle of Sirhind</strong> (June 22, 1555).</li>\n                <li><strong>Restoration:</strong> Humayun triumphantly re-entered Delhi on July 23, 1555, restoring the Mughal dynasty after 15 years of exile.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. Tragic Death at Sher Mandal (January 1556 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>On January 24, 1556, while descending the steep, polished stone steps of his octagonal library tower, the <strong>Sher Mandal</strong> (within Delhi’s Purana Qila), with an armful of books, Humayun heard the Muezzin's evening call to prayer (<em>Azan</em>).</li>\n                <li>Attempting to bow in reverence, his foot caught in his flowing royal robe (<em>qaba</em>), causing him to tumble down the stairs; he succumbed to severe head injuries three days later on January 27, 1556.</li>\n                <li>The eminent British historian <strong>Stanley Lane-Poole</strong> famously remarked: <em>\"Humayun tumbled through life, and he tumbled out of it.\"</em></li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
