            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Sher Shah Suri: Early Life, Rise in Bihar & Afghan Restoration</h3>\n            <p>Sher Shah Suri was one of the greatest administrative and military geniuses of medieval India. In a brief reign of only five years (1540–1545), he rebuilt northern India's fiscal, agrarian, and military structures, laying the bedrock later perfected by Akbar.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Early Life and Lineage</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Lineage:</strong> Born as <strong>Farid Khan</strong> around 1486 (or 1472) at Narnaul or Bajwara (Punjab), he belonged to the Sur sub-tribe of the Pashtun Afghans. His grandfather Ibrahim Khan Sur and father Hasan Khan Sur held jagirs in <strong>Sasaram</strong>, Khawaspur, and Tanda in south Bihar.</li>\n                <li><strong>Administrative Apprenticeship:</strong> Estranged from his father due to his stepmother’s jealousy, Farid went to Jaunpur (the celebrated center of Islamic learning, known as the <em>Shiraz of the East</em>), where he mastered Persian literature, Islamic jurisprudence, and history. Impressed by his brilliance, his father appointed him manager of the family jagir at Sasaram (1497–1518), where Farid gained foundational grassroots experience in agrarian revenue assessment and curbing corrupt zamindars.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Title 'Sher Khan' and Rise in Bihar</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Service under Bahar Khan Lohani:</strong> Farid entered the service of Bahar Khan Lohani (Sultan Muhammad Shah), the independent Afghan governor of Bihar. While accompanying his patron on a hunting expedition, Farid reportedly killed a fully grown tiger single-handedly with a sword stroke, earning the celebrated title <strong>Sher Khan</strong> and the appointment of tutor (<em>ataliq</em>) to Prince Jalal Khan Lohani.</li>\n                <li><strong>Brief Service under Babur:</strong> Seeking wider experience, Sher Khan served Babur in 1527–1528 and observed Mughal military tactics at close quarters. Babur astutely noted his piercing intellect and warned his minister: <em>\"Keep an eye on Sher Khan; he is a man of high genius and marks of royalty shine on his forehead.\"</em></li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Consolidation of Bihar and Bengal</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Acquisition of Chunar (1530 AD):</strong> Sher Khan married <strong>Lad Malika</strong>, the wealthy widow of Taj Khan Sarangkhani (governor of Chunar), thereby acquiring the impregnable hill fort of Chunar and its vast accumulated treasury.</li>\n                <li><strong>Battle of Surajgarh (1534 AD):</strong> Fought near Kiul/Munger in Bihar, Sher Khan defeated the combined armies of the Lohani Afghans and Sultan Mahmud Shah of Bengal. This brilliant tactical victory made Sher Khan the undisputed master of Bihar.</li>\n                <li><strong>Annexation of Bengal (1538 AD):</strong> Sher Khan besieged Gaur and extracted an indemnity of 13 lakh dinars, subsequently occupying Gaur before Humayun could intervene.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
