            "id": "m10",
            "title": "Sher Shah’s Centralized Administration: Sarkars, Parganas & Village Governance",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Sher Shah’s Centralized Administration: Sarkars, Parganas & Village Governance</h3>\n            <p>Sher Shah Suri rejected arbitrary despotic rule in favor of a benevolent, highly organized administrative apparatus that eliminated intermediary corruption, centralized fiscal authority, and held local officials strictly accountable.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Central Executive Organs</h4>\n            <p>Although Sher Shah was an autocrat who personally supervised all state business, he maintained four key imperial ministries (<em>Diwans</em>):</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Diwan-i-Wizarat:</strong> Headed by the <em>Wazir</em>, overseeing imperial revenues, expenditure, and financial accounts.</li>\n                <li><strong>Diwan-i-Ariz:</strong> Headed by the <em>Ariz-i-Mamalik</em>, in charge of military recruitment, troop inspections, salaries, and maintaining the brand on horses (<em>Dagh</em>) and descriptive descriptive rolls of soldiers (<em>Chehra</em>).</li>\n                <li><strong>Diwan-i-Risalat:</strong> Handled diplomatic relations, ambassadors, foreign correspondence, and religious stipends.</li>\n                <li><strong>Diwan-i-Insha:</strong> Headed by the <em>Dabir-i-Khas</em>, responsible for drafting and dispatching royal firmans (decrees) and official state correspondence.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Provincial Restructuring: The Sarkar System</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Sher Shah abolished large semi-independent provincial governorships (subahs) to prevent regional rebellions, dividing his entire empire into <strong>47 administrative divisions called <em>Sarkars</em></strong> (districts). In Bengal, he created 19 Sarkars under non-military civilian oversight headed by <strong>Qazi Fazilat</strong> (designated as <em>Amin-i-Bangla</em>).</li>\n                <li>Each <em>Sarkar</em> was administered by two chief imperial officers:\n                    <ol>\n                        <li><strong>Shiqdar-i-Shiqdaran (Chief Shiqdar):</strong> An executive military officer maintaining law and order, commanding the local garrison, and supervising parganas.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Munsif-i-Munsifan (Chief Munsif):</strong> A judicial and civil magistrate responsible for supervising revenue assessments and adjudicating civil/revenue disputes.</li>\n                    </ol>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The Pargana and Village Machinery</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Each <em>Sarkar</em> was subdivided into numerous <strong>Parganas</strong> (sub-districts). A Pargana was staffed by four primary officials:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Shiqdar:</strong> Executive officer with military police powers.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Amil (or Munsif):</strong> Revenue assessment and collection officer.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Photadar (or Khazanadar):</strong> Pargana treasurer.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Two Karkuns:</strong> Clerks who recorded revenue accounts—one in <strong>Persian</strong> and the other in the local vernacular <strong>Hindi</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Village Governance & Local Responsibility:</strong> The lowest administrative unit was the village (<em>Gram</em>), autonomous under the village headman (<strong>Muqaddam</strong> or <em>Mukhiya</em>) and the village accountant (<strong>Patwari</strong>).</li>\n                <li><strong>Principle of Local Accountability:</strong> Sher Shah introduced the strict principle of collective local responsibility for crime: if a robbery or murder took place within a village territory, the <em>Muqaddam</em> was bound to produce the culprit or pay full compensation from his own pocket. If he failed, he was held criminally liable. As historian Abbas Khan Sarwani observed in <em>Tarikh-i-Sher Shahi</em>, an old woman with a basket of gold ornaments could travel safely without fear of robbery.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Sher Shah divided his empire into 47 Sarkars, each administered by a Shiqdar-i-Shiqdaran (executive/military) and a Munsif-i-Munsifan (revenue/civil justice).",
                "In Bengal, Sher Shah appointed Qazi Fazilat as Amin-i-Bangla to maintain civilian administrative harmony.",
                "Each Pargana had a Shiqdar, an Amil (revenue), a Photadar (treasurer), and two Karkuns (writing accounts in Persian and Hindi).",
                "Sher Shah enforced the doctrine of local responsibility: village Muqaddams were held personally liable to produce robbers or pay compensation.",
                "Abbas Khan Sarwani's Tarikh-i-Sher Shahi praised Sher Shah's flawless public security."
            ],
