            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Akbar’s Religious Evolution: Ibadat Khana, Mahzar, Sulh-i-Kul & Din-i-Ilahi</h3>\n            <p>Akbar’s spiritual quest transformed him from an orthodox Sunni prince into an enlightened cosmopolitan philosopher-king whose policy of <strong>Sulh-i-Kul (Universal Peace)</strong> remains one of the finest intellectual achievements in world history.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Ibadat Khana at Fatehpur Sikri (1575–1578 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Construction in 1575:</strong> Following the conquest of Gujarat and the birth of Salim, Akbar constructed the <strong>Ibadat Khana (House of Worship)</strong> at his new red sandstone capital, Fatehpur Sikri.</li>\n                <li><strong>Initial Orthodox Phase:</strong> Initially, debates were restricted to Muslim scholars—Sunni ulema, sheikhs, and sayyids. However, Akbar was deeply disillusioned by their petty sectarian squabbles, mutual excommunications (takfir), and greed for land grants.</li>\n                <li><strong>Universal Opening (1578 AD):</strong> In 1578, Akbar opened the doors of the Ibadat Khana to thinkers of all religions:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Hindus:</strong> Purushottam and Devi (who taught Akbar the concepts of reincarnation and Maya).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Jains:</strong> Acharya <strong>Hiravijaya Suri</strong> (whom Akbar honored with the supreme title <em>Jagadguru</em>, 'World Teacher'), Vijayasena Suri, and Bhanuchandra Upadhyaya. Impressed by Jain philosophy, Akbar prohibited animal slaughter on designated holy days.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Parsis (Zoroastrians):</strong> Dastur <strong>Meherji Rana</strong> of Navsari (Gujarat), who explained sacred fire rituals; Akbar adopted the perpetual sacred fire at court and performed sun worship.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Christians:</strong> Three Jesuit missions from Goa, led by <strong>Father Rodolfo Acquaviva</strong> and <strong>Father Antonio Monserrate</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Mahzar / Infallibility Decree (September 1579 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Drafted by the liberal philosopher <strong>Sheikh Mubarak</strong> (father of Abul Fazl and Faizi) and signed reluctantly by the chief ulema (including Makhdum-ul-Mulk and Abdun Nabi).</li>\n                <li><strong>Primacy of the Sovereign:</strong> The decree proclaimed Akbar as the <strong>Imam-i-Adil</strong> (Just Supreme Ruler) and <strong>Amir-ul-Muminin</strong>. It stipulated that if legal/theological jurists differed on any point of Islamic law, Akbar’s decree had ultimate supremacy, provided it was in accordance with the Quran and promoted the public welfare.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Sulh-i-Kul and the Tauhid-i-Ilahi / Din-i-Ilahi (1582 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Sulh-i-Kul (Universal Peace / Absolute Tolerance):</strong> Formulated by Abul Fazl, it declared that the sovereign was the father of all his subjects irrespective of creed, guaranteeing complete freedom of worship.</li>\n                <li><strong>Din-i-Ilahi (Divine Monotheism):</strong> In 1582, Akbar instituted an elite, ethical socio-spiritual fraternity named <strong>Tauhid-i-Ilahi</strong> (Divine Monotheism), mislabeled by European historians as a 'new religion'.\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>It possessed no dogmas, no holy book, no priesthood, and no temples.</li>\n                        <li>Members greeted each other with <em>'Allahu Akbar'</em> ('God is Most Great') and responded with <em>'Jalla Jalaluhu'</em> ('May His Glory Shine').</li>\n                        <li>It was entirely voluntary; only 18 prominent court nobles joined, with <strong>Raja Birbal</strong> being the only Hindu noble to formally enroll. Raja Man Singh famously declined, stating his loyalty was political, not theological.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. The Navaratnas and Architecture of Fatehpur Sikri</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Navaratnas (Nine Gems):</strong> Raja Birbal (Mahesh Das), Raja Todar Mal, Raja Man Singh, Tansen (Ramtanu Pandey, musical genius), Abul Fazl (historian, author of <em>Akbarnama</em>), Faizi (poet laureate), Abdur Rahim Khan-i-Khanan, Mullah Do-Piyaza, and Fakir Aziao-Din.</li>\n                <li><strong>Architectural Triumphs:</strong> Akbar founded <strong>Fatehpur Sikri</strong> in 1571. Its crowning monuments include the monumental <strong>Buland Darwaza</strong> (erected in 1601 to commemorate his Gujarat conquest), the marble <strong>Tomb of Sheikh Salim Chishti</strong>, the pillared <strong>Panch Mahal</strong>, the Diwan-i-Khas (with its magnificent central lotus pillar), and Jodha Bai's Palace.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
